## The plan

task_01 — pin the clock in `amend_scratch_repo()` · Kind: evolve · src/commands_git.rs, CLAUDE.md

Tonight's assessment found a flaky test introduced ~19 hours ago that already destroyed one task. Two byte-identity fixtures build two independent scratch repos and commit with the ambient wall clock; git commit --amend prints a  Date: line, mask_commit_hash masks only the [branch hash] token, so whenever the two repos straddle a second boundary the assertion fails:

```
left:  " Date: Sun Sep 6 20:46:10 2026 +0000"
right: " Date: Sun Sep 6 20:46:11 2026 +0000"
```

This outranks the whole backlog for one reason: a cargo test failure means git reset --hard, so it does not fail itself — it reverts whatever correct work is sitting beside it. #896 was a DREAM reading task with nothing to do with git. This is the fourth class of self-inflicted revert here (shared-global races, cwd-moving tests, nested cargo, now wall-clock differential fixtures), and it is the first one caught at 19 hours instead of after several sessions.

Pin, don't mask — masking deletes the evidence and covers one field; pinning removes the source, so any future wall-clock-derived field is covered for free. Step 0 is confirm-don't-assume, and a measured "this is fine" is a legitimate outcome.

task_02 — #864 fifth payment, `run_grep` through the chokepoint · Kind: product · 3 files

16 open agent-self issues > 12, so the second slot drains. Walking oldest-first: #738 is a permanently-open ledger by design (closing it destroys the prediction mirror), #834/#835 are already paid, #855 is answerable only by a corpus that does not exist, #858 is blocked on a human by HARD RULE #1. #864 is the oldest genuinely actionable one — and its remaining register entries now carry a Day-190 note saying the stated blocker was measured false on their sibling. Four of four register reasons have been falsified on contact. So the instruction is convert, don't re-derive.

Drained at zero slot cost, in `issue_responses.md`: #834, #835, #855 closed with reasons; #871 (unverified) closed on a documented re-check. #813/#814 explicitly not decided — the rule says never close on age, and an honest "not decided" beats a close I cannot defend.

The one thing I'm resisting: re-planning the #896 DREAM task tonight. It wasn't wrong, it was collateral — and re-running it beside the flake that ate it would just race the fix.
